Optimal Solution - Use dynamic programming to compute the minimum number of coins needed to form each amount up to the target.
                    This is an unbounded knapsack problem where each coin can be used unlimited times, solved using bottom-up DP.

class Solution {
    public int coinChange(int[] coins, int amount) {
        // If amount is 0, no coins are needed
        if (amount < 1) return 0;
        // minDp[i] = minimum number of coins needed to make amount i
        int[] minDp = new int[amount + 1];
        // Build the DP table from 1 to amount
        for (int i = 1; i <= amount; i++) {
            // Initialize with a large value (unreachable state)
            minDp[i] = Integer.MAX_VALUE;
            // Try every coin
            for (int coin : coins) {
                // Check if coin can be used
                if (coin <= i && minDp[i - coin] != Integer.MAX_VALUE) {
                    // Take the minimum coins needed
                    minDp[i] = Math.min(minDp[i], 1 + minDp[i - coin]);
                }
            }
        }
        // If amount cannot be formed, return -1
        return minDp[amount] == Integer.MAX_VALUE ? -1 : minDp[amount];
    }
}


Time Complexity :- O(amount x n).
Space Complexity :- O(amount).
